Sentences with "I wish," "he/she wishes," "as if," "as though" — expressing an unfulfilled wish or an imaginary/unreal situation — take Simple Past.

The Structure

I wish / He wishes / as if / as though + subject + Simple Past

Examples

(a) I wish I was / were a doctor.
✅ I wish I were a doctor.

You're not actually a doctor — it's a wish about an unreal present situation.
With "wish/as if/as though," the verb "be" is a special case: formally, always use "were" (not "was") for all subjects — I were, he were, she were — even though this breaks normal subject-verb agreement. This is called the subjunctive mood.

(b) If she was / were a bird, she would fly.
✅ If she were a bird, she would fly.

Same subjunctive rule — since it's clearly unreal (she can never be a bird), "were" is used regardless of "she" being singular.

(c) He behaves as if he knows / knew everything.
✅ He behaves as if he knew everything.

He doesn't actually know everything — it's a false impression he gives off. Simple past ("knew") signals the unreality.


Bonus: Wishes about the PAST (not covered in Rule 17 itself, but commonly tested alongside it)

If the wish is about something that already happened (a past regret) — use Past Perfect, not simple past:

I wish I had studied harder. (regret about the past — not "studied")
She wishes she had gone to the party.

Quick distinction:

Unreal present → wish + simple past (I wish I knew)
Unreal past (regret) → wish + past perfect (I wish I had known)
